        "subject": "LU-20772 socklnd: remove broken TCP backoff support",
        "message": "LU-20772 socklnd: remove broken TCP backoff support\n\nThe TCP_BACKOFF_INIT and TCP_BACKOFF_MAX socket options were never\npart of mainline Linux. They come from the \"Customizable TCP backoff\npatch\" that Ben Woodard and Brian Behlendorf posted to netdev in\nSeptember 2006 for LLNL's oversubscribed gigE clusters, where\nthousands of TCP streams backed off in lock step:\n\n  https://lwn.net/Articles/202398/\n\nNo kernel defines the options, so the configure grep for TCP_BACKOFF\nnever succeeds and SOCKNAL_BACKOFF has been compiled out on every\nbuild for years. Even if a kernel did define the options, the code\nwould no longer build: it calls kernel_setsockopt() directly, which\nwas removed in Linux 5.8, and the block was never converted to\nsockptr_t like the neighbouring keepalive and IPv6 code.\n\nRemove the LN_CONFIG_BACKOFF configure macro and --disable-backoff\nswitch, the backoff_init and backoff_max module parameters, the\ntunable pointers, the setsockopt block, and the backoff/nobackoff\nDebian build profiles.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial\nSigned-off-by: Timothy Day <timday@thelustrecollective.com>\nChange-Id: I349532d969a17a7f7013b40d4f5a9e7b6a6a6964\nReviewed-on: https://review.whamcloud.com/c/fs/lustre-release/+/69024\nReviewed-by: Brian Behlendorf <behlendorf1@llnl.gov>\nReviewed-by: James Simmons <jsimmons@infradead.org>\nReviewed-by: Oleg Drokin <green@whamcloud.com>\nTested-by: jenkins <devops@whamcloud.com>\nTested-by: Maloo <maloo@whamcloud.com>\nReviewed-by: Chris Horn <chorn@ddn.com>\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
  },
